Chapter: DataTable
  cites Foreword chapter Sort
  cites Foreword chapter Collate
  cites Foreword chapter StringUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Parse
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Layout
  cites UI chapter KeyInput

Section: Column

  TableColumn = record {
    tc-id : Text,
    tc-label : Text,
    tc-width : Integer,
    tc-sortable : Boolean,
    tc-align : Integer
  }

  table-column : Text, Text, Integer -> TableColumn
  table-column (id) (label) (width) = TableColumn {
    tc-id = id,
    tc-label = label,
    tc-width = width,
    tc-sortable = True,
    tc-align = 0
  }

  table-column-fixed : Text, Text, Integer -> TableColumn
  table-column-fixed (id) (label) (width) = TableColumn {
    tc-id = id,
    tc-label = label,
    tc-width = width,
    tc-sortable = False,
    tc-align = 0
  }

Section: Row

  TableRow = record {
    tr-id : Text,
    tr-cells : List Text,
    tr-cell-count : Integer,
    tr-selected : Boolean
  }

  table-row : Text, List Text -> TableRow
  table-row (id) (cells) = TableRow {
    tr-id = id,
    tr-cells = cells,
    tr-cell-count = list-length cells,
    tr-selected = False
  }

Section: State

  DataTableState = record {
    dt-id : Text,
    dt-columns : List TableColumn,
    dt-col-count : Integer,
    dt-rows : List TableRow,
    dt-row-count : Integer,
    dt-sort-col : Integer,
    dt-sort-asc : Boolean,
    dt-selected-row : Integer,
    dt-multi-select : Boolean,
    dt-page-size : Integer,
    dt-page : Integer,
    dt-stripe : Boolean
  }

  data-table-new : Text, List TableColumn, List TableRow -> DataTableState
  data-table-new (id) (cols) (rows) = DataTableState {
    dt-id = id,
    dt-columns = cols,
    dt-col-count = list-length cols,
    dt-rows = rows,
    dt-row-count = list-length rows,
    dt-sort-col = -1,
    dt-sort-asc = True,
    dt-selected-row = -1,
    dt-multi-select = False,
    dt-page-size = 20,
    dt-page = 0,
    dt-stripe = True
  }

Section: Actions

  data-table-sort : DataTableState, Integer -> DataTableState
  data-table-sort (dt) (col-idx) =
    if col-idx < 0 then dt
    else if col-idx >= dt.dt-col-count then dt
    else let col = list-at (dt.dt-columns) col-idx
    in if col.tc-sortable then
      let asc = if dt.dt-sort-col == col-idx then if dt.dt-sort-asc then False else True else True
      in let sorted = dt-sort-rows (dt.dt-rows) col-idx asc (dt.dt-row-count)
      in __record-set (__record-set (__record-set dt "dt-rows" sorted) "dt-sort-col" col-idx) "dt-sort-asc" asc
    else dt

 The sort writes through list-set-at, which stores into the slot and
 hands back the same list, so it is copied first. Without the copy the
 state handed in is sorted too, and a caller holding the unsorted table
 finds it reordered underneath: this function's own type says it returns
 a new state, and the single-select path already copies for the same
 reason.

 A collation key is extracted once per row and the sort compares those,
 which is what the key design is for: the rank tables are consulted once
 per character per row rather than twice per comparison, and the
 comparison stays the `text-compare` this file already used.

  dt-sort-rows : List TableRow, Integer, Boolean, Integer -> List TableRow
  dt-sort-rows (rows) (col) (asc) (n) =
    if n <= 1 then rows
    else let copied = dt-copy-rows rows 0 n []
    in let keys = dt-key-rows copied col 0 n []
    in dt-isort copied keys asc 1 n

  dt-copy-rows : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, List TableRow -> List TableRow
  dt-copy-rows (rows) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dt-copy-rows rows (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at rows i))

  dt-key-rows : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  dt-key-rows (rows) (col) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let k = collate-key (dt-cell-at (list-at rows i) col)
    in dt-key-rows rows col (i + 1) n (list-push acc k)

  dt-isort : List TableRow, List Text, Boolean, Integer, Integer -> List TableRow
  dt-isort (rows) (keys) (asc) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then rows
    else dt-isort (dt-insert rows keys asc i) keys asc (i + 1) n

 Two cells are ordered with text-compare and never with the ordering
 operators. Measured 2026-07-27 against the depot seed: the ordering
 operators on Text do not read the Text at all. They compare the operands
 as they were handed over rather than by content, so two freshly built
 literals answer by the order they were built in and give the same answer
 whatever the letters are: "a" is reported less than "b", "b" less than
 "a", and "a" less than "a", all True. text-compare is the primitive that
 reads the characters, and it is what SkipListText and RankedTextSet
 already order by.

 **text-compare orders by CCE code point, and that is not the alphabet.**
 CCE numbers the letters by English frequency, so e is 13, t is 14, a is
 15, d is 22, c is 24, b is 32 and z is 38, and a column sorted through it
 comes out in an order a human reads as arbitrary. That is the right order
 for an ordered container and the wrong one for a column somebody reads.

 So the cells are not compared directly at all. `Foreword chapter Collate`
 turns each into a sort key whose ENCODING order is collation order, the
 keys are extracted once per row, and `text-compare` over two keys is then
 the alphabetical comparison this column wants.

 The two lists move together. `list-set-at` writes the slot and hands
 back the same list, so swapping the keys is what keeps a row and its key
 at the same index; separating them by one swap would sort the rows by
 somebody else's key and the answer would look almost right.

  dt-insert : List TableRow, List Text, Boolean, Integer -> List TableRow
  dt-insert (rows) (keys) (asc) (i) =
    if i <= 0 then rows
    else let cur = list-at rows i
    in let prev = list-at rows (i - 1)
    in let kcur = list-at keys i
    in let kprev = list-at keys (i - 1)
    in let ord = text-compare kcur kprev
    in let swap = if asc then ord < 0 else ord > 0
    in if swap then dt-insert (dt-swap rows keys i cur prev kcur kprev) keys asc (i - 1)
    else rows

  dt-swap : List TableRow, List Text, Integer, TableRow, TableRow, Text, Text -> List TableRow
  dt-swap (rows) (keys) (i) (cur) (prev) (kcur) (kprev) =
    let moved = list-set-at (list-set-at keys i kprev) (i - 1) kcur
    in list-set-at (list-set-at rows i prev) (i - 1) cur

  dt-cell-at : TableRow, Integer -> Text
  dt-cell-at (row) (col) =
    if col < 0 then ""
    else if col >= row.tr-cell-count then ""
    else list-at (row.tr-cells) col

  data-table-select-row : DataTableState, Integer -> DataTableState
  data-table-select-row (dt) (idx) =
    if idx < 0 then dt
    else if idx >= dt.dt-row-count then dt
    else let cleared = if dt.dt-multi-select then dt.dt-rows else dt-clear-rows (dt.dt-rows) (dt.dt-row-count)
    in let row = list-at cleared idx
    in let toggled = __record-set row "tr-selected" (if row.tr-selected then False else True)
    in __record-set (__record-set dt "dt-rows" (list-set-at cleared idx toggled)) "dt-selected-row" idx

  dt-clear-rows : List TableRow, Integer -> List TableRow
  dt-clear-rows (rows) (n) = dt-clear-loop rows 0 n []

  dt-clear-loop : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, List TableRow -> List TableRow
  dt-clear-loop (rows) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dt-clear-loop rows (i + 1) n (list-push acc (__record-set (list-at rows i) "tr-selected" False))

  data-table-select-all : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  data-table-select-all (dt) =
    __record-set dt "dt-rows" (dt-set-all-selected (dt.dt-rows) (dt.dt-row-count) True)

  data-table-deselect-all : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  data-table-deselect-all (dt) =
    __record-set dt "dt-rows" (dt-set-all-selected (dt.dt-rows) (dt.dt-row-count) False)

  dt-set-all-selected : List TableRow, Integer, Boolean -> List TableRow
  dt-set-all-selected (rows) (n) (val) = dt-set-sel-loop rows 0 n val []

  dt-set-sel-loop : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, Boolean, List TableRow -> List TableRow
  dt-set-sel-loop (rows) (i) (n) (val) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dt-set-sel-loop rows (i + 1) n val (list-push acc (__record-set (list-at rows i) "tr-selected" val))

Section: Pagination

 A table with no rows still has the page the reader is looking at, so the
 count is one rather than zero. Left at zero the footer reads "Page 1 of
 0", and the page a caller is on is outside the range its own count
 declares.

 A page size of twenty is a guess about a window nobody measured, and a
 table whose page is larger than its box does not clip: it lays every row
 out and the surplus lands on the header and on whatever sits below. Nine
 rows in the tracker's pane already covered "ID" and cut "Priori"; fourteen
 were unreadable, and the footer said "Page 1 of 1" the whole time, which is
 true and useless (`works-backlog.md` WORKS-23).

 These two numbers are the terminal theme's, measured through `widget-layout`
 rather than derived, and they move with the widget builder below and with
 the theme's edges. A row panel holding cells that declare 16 comes out 38
 tall including its margin; the header and footer panels holding buttons that
 declare 24 come out 46 each, and with the table panel's own 14 of margin,
 border and padding that is 106. `codex/test/ui/widget-panel-min` prints all
 three from a laid-out tree.

  data-table-row-h : Integer = 38

  data-table-chrome-h : Integer = 106

 The caller passes the height the TABLE was given, not the pane's, and gets
 back a state whose page fits it. Measure that height from a laid-out tree
 rather than deriving it: the layout engine already answered, and a second
 derivation is a second answer to keep in step.

  data-table-rows-that-fit : Integer -> Integer
  data-table-rows-that-fit (table-h) =
    let body = table-h - data-table-chrome-h
    in if body < data-table-row-h then 1 else body / data-table-row-h

 A page size is never zero: at zero `data-table-page-count` answers one and
 `data-table-visible-rows` answers none, so the table would report a page it
 cannot show. One row is the smallest honest answer.

  data-table-fit : DataTableState, Integer -> DataTableState
  data-table-fit (dt) (table-h) =
    let size = data-table-rows-that-fit table-h
    in let sized = __record-set dt "dt-page-size" size
    in let last = data-table-page-count sized - 1
    in if sized.dt-page > last then __record-set sized "dt-page" last else sized

  data-table-page-count : DataTableState -> Integer
  data-table-page-count (dt) =
    if dt.dt-page-size <= 0 then 1
    else if dt.dt-row-count <= 0 then 1
    else let full = dt.dt-row-count / dt.dt-page-size
    in if full * dt.dt-page-size < dt.dt-row-count then full + 1 else full

  data-table-page-next : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  data-table-page-next (dt) =
    let max-page = data-table-page-count dt - 1
    in let next = dt.dt-page + 1
    in if next > max-page then dt
    else __record-set dt "dt-page" next

  data-table-page-prev : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  data-table-page-prev (dt) =
    if dt.dt-page <= 0 then dt
    else __record-set dt "dt-page" (dt.dt-page - 1)

  data-table-visible-rows : DataTableState -> List TableRow
  data-table-visible-rows (dt) =
    let start = dt.dt-page * dt.dt-page-size
    in let end-idx = start + dt.dt-page-size
    in let actual-end = if end-idx > dt.dt-row-count then dt.dt-row-count else end-idx
    in dt-slice-rows (dt.dt-rows) start actual-end []

  dt-slice-rows : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, List TableRow -> List TableRow
  dt-slice-rows (rows) (i) (end-idx) (acc) =
    if i >= end-idx then acc
    else dt-slice-rows rows (i + 1) end-idx (list-push acc (list-at rows i))

Section: Column Width

 A column is as wide as the wider of what its caller asked for and what its
 own header needs, because `tc-width` is a number a pane picked and the
 header text is a fact. The tracker asked for 68 for "Priority", whose button
 needs 80, and drew "Priori" (WORKS-47).

 The sort indicator is reserved whether or not this column is the sorted one,
 and it has to be: `dt-header-cols` appends " ^" to the label of the sorted
 column, so a width computed from the label alone is 16 short of the button
 it is applied to and cuts the indicator off the one column the reader is
 asking about. Reserving it also keeps the columns still when they sort.

  data-table-sort-indicator-w : Integer = 16

  dt-col-width : TableColumn -> Integer
  dt-col-width (col) =
    box-max (col.tc-width) ((widget-button "" (col.tc-label)).wn-min-w + data-table-sort-indicator-w)

  dt-cols-width : List TableColumn, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dt-cols-width (cols) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dt-cols-width cols (i + 1) n (acc + dt-col-width (list-at cols i))

 Every column keeps its floor, which is the header's own text: below that the
 column names nothing and a reader cannot tell which one they are looking at.

 Above the floors the space is taken from the WIDEST column first, by finding
 the largest cap that fits and giving every column the lesser of its width
 and that cap. Sharing the shortfall out in proportion instead takes a little
 from every column, and a little is what an identifier cannot spare: at a
 550-wide budget the proportional rule cut "ID" from 68 to 51 and "CDX-142"
 drew as "CDX...", while the cap comes out at 76 and takes the whole
 shortfall out of "Title", which is the column with something to give.

 Scaling every column by one ratio does not even fit: a column already at its
 floor cannot give the ratio back, so the rest do not shrink enough and the
 total lands over the budget. Measured at a 372-wide budget, the even ratio
 came out at 507.

 A table whose columns already fit is returned unchanged, so a caller that
 never asks is a caller nothing moves for. A budget too small even for the
 floors puts every column at its floor and the table still overruns, which is
 the honest answer: six columns that each name themselves do not fit, and
 only the caller can decide whether to drop one.

 The width passed is the TABLE's own laid-out width, so the caller reads it
 from the same measured node it already reads the height from and pays for no
 second layout. `flex-col-place` gives the header panel the table's width
 whatever its columns come to, which is what makes one number enough.

 The three subtractions are what stands between that width and the columns,
 all measured under the terminal theme and moving with `data-table-row-h`:
 28 for the table panel's own margin, border and padding and then the header
 panel's again inside it, the margin every column carries and
 `flex-row-place` adds, and the gaps between them. Leaving the column margins
 out overran a 550 box by 9.

  data-table-chrome-w : Integer = 28

  data-table-col-gutter : Integer = 4

  data-table-fit-cols : DataTableState, Integer -> DataTableState
  data-table-fit-cols (dt) (table-w) =
    let n = dt.dt-col-count
    in if n <= 0 then dt
    else let gaps = if n > 1 then n - 1 else 0
    in let avail = table-w - data-table-chrome-w - n * data-table-col-gutter - gaps
    in let want = dt-cols-width (dt.dt-columns) 0 n 0
    in if want <= avail then dt
    else let cap = dt-cap-for (dt.dt-columns) n avail 0 want
    in let scaled = dt-scale-cols (dt.dt-columns) cap 0 n []
    in __record-set dt "dt-columns" scaled

 The cap is found by halving rather than by walking the pixels: the sum at a
 given cap rises with the cap, so the largest one that fits is a binary
 search, and the interval strictly shrinks every step.

  dt-cap-for : List TableColumn, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dt-cap-for (cols) (n) (avail) (lo) (hi) =
    if lo >= hi then lo
    else let mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2
    in if dt-cols-at-cap cols 0 n mid 0 <= avail then dt-cap-for cols n avail mid hi
    else dt-cap-for cols n avail lo (mid - 1)

  dt-col-at-cap : TableColumn, Integer -> Integer
  dt-col-at-cap (col) (cap) = box-min (dt-col-width col) (box-max (dt-col-floor col) cap)

  dt-cols-at-cap : List TableColumn, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dt-cols-at-cap (cols) (i) (n) (cap) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dt-cols-at-cap cols (i + 1) n cap (acc + dt-col-at-cap (list-at cols i) cap)

  dt-col-floor : TableColumn -> Integer
  dt-col-floor (col) = (widget-button "" (col.tc-label)).wn-min-w + data-table-sort-indicator-w

  dt-cols-floor : List TableColumn, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dt-cols-floor (cols) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dt-cols-floor cols (i + 1) n (acc + dt-col-floor (list-at cols i))

 A scaled column is a NEW record. `__record-set` stores in place and hands
 back the same one, so writing the width through it would narrow the caller's
 own columns, and a caller holding a shared column list would find every
 table built from it narrowed too. `data-table-sort` copies its rows for the
 same reason.

  dt-scale-cols : List TableColumn, Integer, Integer, Integer, List TableColumn -> List TableColumn
  dt-scale-cols (cols) (cap) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let col = list-at cols i
    in let shrunk = dt-col-at-cap col cap
    in let narrowed = TableColumn {
      tc-id = col.tc-id,
      tc-label = col.tc-label,
      tc-width = shrunk,
      tc-sortable = col.tc-sortable,
      tc-align = col.tc-align
    }
    in dt-scale-cols cols cap (i + 1) n (list-push acc narrowed)

Section: Widget Building

  data-table-widget : DataTableState -> WidgetNode
  data-table-widget (dt) =
    let header = dt-header-widget dt
    in let vis = data-table-visible-rows dt
    in let body-rows = dt-body-widgets dt vis 0 (list-length vis) (dt.dt-page * dt.dt-page-size) []
    in let footer = dt-footer-widget dt
    in widget-panel (dt.dt-id) DirColumn 0 ([header] & body-rows & [footer])

  dt-header-widget : DataTableState -> WidgetNode
  dt-header-widget (dt) =
    let cols = dt-header-cols dt 0 (dt.dt-col-count) []
    in widget-panel (dt.dt-id & "-hdr") DirRow 1 cols

  dt-header-cols : DataTableState, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  dt-header-cols (dt) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let col = list-at (dt.dt-columns) i
    in let indicator = if dt.dt-sort-col == i then if dt.dt-sort-asc then " ^" else " v" else ""
    in let btn = widget-button (dt.dt-id & "-col-" & show i) (col.tc-label & indicator)
    in let sized = widget-set-min btn (dt-col-width col) 24
    in dt-header-cols dt (i + 1) n (list-push acc sized)

  dt-body-widgets : DataTableState, List TableRow, Integer, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  dt-body-widgets (dt) (rows) (i) (n) (base-idx) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let row = list-at rows i
    in let row-widget = dt-row-widget dt row (base-idx + i) i
    in dt-body-widgets dt rows (i + 1) n base-idx (list-push acc row-widget)

 A stripe is decoration and a selection is state, so the selection wins.
 The stripe used to be applied last and unconditionally, which set the
 hover state over the pressed state a selected row had just been given:
 selecting an odd row looked exactly like selecting nothing, on every
 other row, while the row's own tr-selected said otherwise.

  dt-row-widget : DataTableState, TableRow, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  dt-row-widget (dt) (row) (abs-idx) (vis-idx) =
    let cells = dt-cell-widgets dt row 0 (dt.dt-col-count) []
    in let row-w = widget-panel (dt.dt-id & "-row-" & show abs-idx) DirRow 1 cells
    in if row.tr-selected then widget-set-state row-w state-pressed
    else if dt.dt-stripe then
      let even = vis-idx - (vis-idx / 2) * 2
      in if even == 0 then row-w else widget-set-state row-w state-hover
    else row-w

  dt-cell-widgets : DataTableState, TableRow, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  dt-cell-widgets (dt) (row) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let col = list-at (dt.dt-columns) i
    in let txt = dt-elide (dt-cell-at row i) (dt-col-width col)
    in let lbl = widget-label (dt.dt-id & "-cell-" & row.tr-id & "-" & show i) txt
    in let sized = widget-set-min lbl (dt-col-width col) 16
    in dt-cell-widgets dt row (i + 1) n (list-push acc sized)

 A cell wider than its column is CUT by whatever draws it, and a cut that
 says nothing is worse than a short value: at a column fitted to 51, "CDX-142"
 draws as "CDX-14", which is not a truncation a reader can see, it is a
 different issue number. So the text is elided here, where the width is
 known, and the reader is told.

 The glyph width is asked of the widget library rather than written down,
 since it is `widget-label`'s own arithmetic and a table that disagreed with
 it would elide at the wrong place.

  dt-glyph-w : Integer
  dt-glyph-w = (widget-label "" "M").wn-min-w

 What a cell can DRAW is its width less its own margin, which
 `box-content-from-style` takes off again inside the rect `flex-row-place`
 already stripped it from. Eliding against the full width put the last glyph
 half outside the box, so a cut cell ended in ".." and the marker read as
 something shorter than it was.

  dt-elide : Text, Integer -> Text
  dt-elide (txt) (width) =
    let drawable = width - data-table-col-gutter
    in let fits = if dt-glyph-w <= 0 then text-length txt else drawable / dt-glyph-w
    in if text-length txt <= fits then txt
    else if fits <= 3 then text-take txt (box-max 0 fits)
    else text-take txt (fits - 3) & "..."

  dt-footer-widget : DataTableState -> WidgetNode
  dt-footer-widget (dt) =
    let page-count = data-table-page-count dt
    in let info = widget-label (dt.dt-id & "-info") ("Page " & show (dt.dt-page + 1) & " of " & show page-count & " | " & show (dt.dt-row-count) & " rows")
    in let prev-btn = widget-button (dt.dt-id & "-prev") "< Prev"
    in let next-btn = widget-button (dt.dt-id & "-next") "Next >"
    in widget-panel (dt.dt-id & "-footer") DirRow 8 [prev-btn, info, next-btn]

Section: Answering A Click

 WORKS-13 asked for per-row widget ids so that a click could name a row.
 They were already here: `dt-row-widget` names each row `<id>-row-<n>` with
 the ABSOLUTE row index, and `dt-cell-widgets` names each cell
 `<id>-cell-<row key>-<column>`. What was missing is the other end, a way to
 read one back, and every pane that wanted it was left doing string surgery
 or re-deriving the layout's own arithmetic.

 `ev-hit-widget` answers with the DEEPEST node under the point, so an
 ordinary click on a row lands on a cell and only a click in the row's own
 padding lands on the row panel. Both shapes are read here for that reason;
 a caller that handled only one would work in testing and miss most clicks.

  dt-row-prefix : DataTableState -> Text
  dt-row-prefix (dt) = dt.dt-id & "-row-"

  dt-cell-prefix : DataTableState -> Text
  dt-cell-prefix (dt) = dt.dt-id & "-cell-"

 The cell id carries the row's KEY, which is the caller's own identifier and
 not an index, so it is looked up rather than parsed as a number. The key
 runs from the prefix to the last hyphen, because a key may itself contain
 one.

  dt-key-end : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dt-key-end (s) (i) (n) (best) =
    if i >= n then best
    else if text-substring s i 1 == "-" then dt-key-end s (i + 1) n i
    else dt-key-end s (i + 1) n best

  dt-index-of-key : List TableRow, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dt-index-of-key (rows) (key) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0 - 1
    else if (list-at rows i).tr-id == key then i
    else dt-index-of-key rows key (i + 1) n

 A row that is not on the visible page still answers, because the id carries
 the absolute index and the key is looked up across every row. A caller that
 wants "only what is on screen" has the page to check against; a helper that
 silently answered -1 for an off-page row would be indistinguishable from a
 miss.

  data-table-row-of-hit : DataTableState, Text -> Integer
  data-table-row-of-hit (dt) (hit-id) =
    if text-starts-with hit-id (dt-row-prefix dt)
      then text-to-int (text-drop hit-id (text-length (dt-row-prefix dt)))
    else if text-starts-with hit-id (dt-cell-prefix dt) then
      let start = text-length (dt-cell-prefix dt)
      in let dash = dt-key-end hit-id start (text-length hit-id) (0 - 1)
      in if dash < 0 then 0 - 1
      else dt-index-of-key (dt.dt-rows) (text-substring hit-id start (dash - start)) 0 (dt.dt-row-count)
    else 0 - 1

Section: Keyboard Handling

 A key is what `UI chapter KeyInput` says it is: a character is its CCE
 code point and a named key is a constant at or above `key-named-base`.
 These arms tested 38, 40, 13, 33 and 34, which are Windows virtual key
 codes and, read as CCE, ordinary letters. Under the contract the quire
 publishes every one of them missed, so a table could not be walked,
 selected from or paged at all: measured against seed 8B7A02EC on a
 twenty-five row two-page table, every key left `dt-selected-row` at -1
 and `dt-page` at 0.

 Enter selects whatever row the navigation has reached, so on a table
 nobody has moved in yet it correctly does nothing: `dt-selected-row`
 starts at -1 and `data-table-select-row` refuses a negative index.

  data-table-handle-key : DataTableState, Integer -> DataTableState
  data-table-handle-key (dt) (key) =
    if key == key-up then dt-nav-up dt
    else if key == key-down then dt-nav-down dt
    else if key == key-enter then data-table-select-row dt (dt.dt-selected-row)
    else if key == key-pgup then data-table-page-prev dt
    else if key == key-pgdn then data-table-page-next dt
    else dt

  dt-nav-up : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  dt-nav-up (dt) =
    let prev = dt.dt-selected-row - 1
    in if prev < 0 then dt
    else __record-set dt "dt-selected-row" prev

  dt-nav-down : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  dt-nav-down (dt) =
    let next = dt.dt-selected-row + 1
    in if next >= dt.dt-row-count then dt
    else __record-set dt "dt-selected-row" next

Section: Formatting

  format-data-table : DataTableState -> Text
  format-data-table (dt) =
    dt.dt-id & " cols=" & show (dt.dt-col-count) & " rows=" & show (dt.dt-row-count)
    & " page=" & show (dt.dt-page + 1) & "/" & show (data-table-page-count dt)

